      "Corrected: the jymphong prompt now follows the East Khasi Hills district description (long, sleeveless, collarless, fastened with thongs in front); the 'deep blue' colour came from a wedding description and was dropped for festival wear.",
      "Confirmed: jainsem to the ankles over a blouse with the tapmohkhlieh; the formal muga silk dhara knotted or pinned at the shoulders; men's jymphong and dhoti for ceremonies; Shad Suk Mynsiem in April over three days (East Khasi Hills district). Kyrshah confirmed as a checked head cloth worn in the harvest season (Pratidin Time), which adds that women also wear it at festivals.",
      "Conflict: Pratidin Time describes the Garo dakmanda as a small cloth wrapped at the waist with a lungi and blouse, while Fibre2Fashion and Caleidoscope describe a lungi-style wrap; the prompt keeps the wrap with a blouse. Pnar thoh khyrwang with a velvet blouse rests on Caleidoscope only."
